### SCENARIO 2 — a concurrent-edit conflict, and the loop that RESOLVES it.
###
### Nothing else in this repository walks the whole loop. Two clients read the same row and hold the
### same version. The first writes and wins. The second writes and is refused — and then does the
### thing `If-Match` exists to make possible: re-reads, sees the first writer's change, merges its own
### on top, and retries with the fresh version.
###
### The payoff is asserted at the end and it is the point of the whole mechanism: the final row
### carries BOTH writers' changes. A lost update would leave it carrying only one.

### The row both clients are about to edit.
# @name CreateContestedRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-8100",
  "title": "Contested job",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 3,
  "quoted_price": 1000.00,
  "access_code": "LOOP",
  "customer_id": "{{CustomerAcmeId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### Client 1 reads and holds a version.
# @name ClientOneReads
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateContestedRow.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### Client 2 reads the same row and holds the SAME version. Nothing has changed yet.
# @name ClientTwoReads
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateContestedRow.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### Client 1 writes first, under the version it holds. It wins.
# @name ClientOneWrites
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateContestedRow.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
If-Match: {{ClientOneReads.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "priority": 1 }

### Client 2 writes under the version IT holds, which the row has since moved past. 412.
### Without the loop below, the usual reaction to this 412 — re-read, re-apply — is what clobbers
### client 1's change, so being refused is only half of the mechanism.
# @name ClientTwoIsRefused
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [412]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateContestedRow.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
If-Match: {{ClientTwoReads.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "in_progress" }

### Client 2 re-reads. This is where it LEARNS about client 1's change rather than overwriting it.
# @name ClientTwoRereads
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateContestedRow.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### Client 2 retries with the fresh version, carrying only its own field. It succeeds.
# @name ClientTwoRetries
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateContestedRow.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
If-Match: {{ClientTwoRereads.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "in_progress" }

### THE STATE OF THE WORLD: both changes are present. Neither writer lost.
# @name FinalContestedRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{CreateContestedRow.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
